Transport	- Supported DTLS versions and ciphers
- Anonymous/weak ciphers enabled
- Certificate validation (if used)
- Replay attack (duplicate DTLS records)	sslscan, nmap --script ssl-enum-ciphers -sU -p 5683 (CoAPs), custom scripts
PSK	- PSK identity enumeration
- PSK hint leakage
- Weak PSK derivation (predictable IDs)
- Forward secrecy (missing ECDHE/DHE)	Write a DTLS client that tries multiple identities
End‑to‑end	- Nonce reuse in COSE/NACL
- Missing context binding (HKDF fields)
- Weak algorithms (RSA1_5, etc.)
- Message replay without sequence number	Analyze protocol messages; replay attacks
COSE/JOSE	- alg: none signature bypass
- Key confusion (RS256→HS256)
- Unprotected header manipulation
- Algorithm downgrade	Standard JWT attacks applied to COSE tokens
Key management	- Hardcoded keys in firmware
- Insecure provisioning (weak enrollment)
- Ratchet implementation flaws
- Lack of zeroization	Firmware extraction, binary analysis, reverse engineering
7. Summary of Critical IoT Vulnerabilities
Vulnerability	Impact	Example
Nonce reuse in GCM/CCM	Complete loss of confidentiality and authentication	Reusing nonce with AES-GCM can leak plaintext and allow forgery
Missing forward secrecy	If long-term key leaks, all past sessions decrypted	PSK without ECDHE
Weak PSK identity	Brute-force leads to unauthorized device connections	Predictable serial‑based identity
COSE algorithm confusion	Token forgery or decryption	Accepting alg: none or using weak key wrapping
End‑to‑end replay	Replay old commands to IoT actuator	No sequence number in encrypted payload
Hardcoded keys in firmware	Mass device compromise	Extracted from firmware dump
IoT security testing often requires specialized hardware and knowledge of embedded systems, but the core cryptographic weaknesses are the same as in web APIs—just with different protocols. Focus on nonce reuse, algorithm downgrades, and missing authentication; those are the highest‑impact bugs you’ll likely find.
